WEBVTT

NOTE Text: the publisher's Official Report, unchanged. Timing: aligned automatically to the audio by signed-track-manifest.

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:10.920
Donald MacKinnon: Last week, the Public
Petitions Committee took the decision to

00:00:10.920 --> 00:00:16.200
keep open petition PE2202, seeking to end
the harvesting of guga from the island of

00:00:16.200 --> 00:00:17.440
Sula Sgeir in my constituency.

00:00:18.280 --> 00:00:21.800
Does the First Minister, like me,
recognise and value the deep cultural

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:25.560
significance of the taking
of guga from Sula Sgeir?

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:29.420
Can he confirm that the position of his
Government remains that it does not intend

00:00:29.420 --> 00:00:35.100
to amend section 16 of the Wildlife and
Countryside Act 1981 to remove the power

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:38.060
to grant licences to take
gannets from Sula Sgeir?

00:00:39.980 --> 00:00:43.600
The First Minister: Before I come to Mr
MacKinnon’s question, I associate myself

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.460
with the comments that the Presiding
Officer made at the start of business this

00:00:46.460 --> 00:00:50.320
afternoon about the sad death of Cathie
Craigie, who was a distinguished

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.320
member of Parliament.

00:00:51.740 --> 00:00:55.920
I express my sympathy to her family and
to her colleagues in the Labour Party and

00:00:55.920 --> 00:00:57.140
across the political spectrum.

00:00:58.040 --> 00:01:01.420
She was a generous and kind lady to
work with in the Scottish Parliament,

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:05.520
and I express my thanks for
the work that she contributed

00:01:05.520 --> 00:01:07.620
to the Parliament.

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:12.920
In relation to Mr MacKinnon’s question,
the Government has no intention of

00:01:12.920 --> 00:01:15.580
changing the provisions in the
Wildlife and Countryside Act 1981.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:21.400
The issues that have been wrestled
with by NatureScot are the

00:01:21.400 --> 00:01:23.220
consideration of scientific evidence.

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:27.040
I understand and appreciate that there
is quite a vigorous debate about that

00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:32.440
scientific evidence, but NatureScot
has fulfilled my expectation that

00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:37.880
it would be open and transparent about
the information that it had considered in

00:01:37.880 --> 00:01:38.920
coming to its conclusion.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.920
It is important for me to
state for the record that,

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:47.260
although a decision was taken in
relation to the guga hunt in 2026,

00:01:47.860 --> 00:01:53.260
that does not prohibit any
application for subsequent years.

00:01:53.700 --> 00:01:58.660
I hope that that clarity assists
Mr MacKinnon’s constituents,

00:01:59.200 --> 00:02:00.380
many of whom I know are troubled by

00:02:06.980 --> 00:02:08.308
the issue.

00:02:08.308 --> 00:02:11.100
Helen McDade: Thank you, Presiding
Officer, and my apologies for earlier.

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:15.020
Considering that there is a public inquiry
into the NHS Tayside practices that

00:02:15.020 --> 00:02:20.120
allowed Sam Eljamel to butcher patients
over nearly 20 years and another public

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:24.400
inquiry into NHS Greater Glasgow and
Clyde’s Queen Elizabeth university

00:02:24.400 --> 00:02:29.180
hospital, can the First Minister explain
how accountability and justice can be

00:02:29.180 --> 00:02:33.960
delivered despite a major reorganisation
of NHS boards that will remove

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:35.120
those entities?

00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:37.420
The First Minister: The public

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:44.700
inquiries will take their course and there
is nothing about the reorganisation of the

00:02:44.700 --> 00:02:47.720
national health service, as proposed
by the programme for government,

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:53.040
that will interrupt or in any way
inhibit the inquiries undertaking their

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.380
full scrutiny. All issues that are
relevant arising out of those inquiries

00:02:58.380 --> 00:03:04.360
into NHS Tayside or NHS Greater
Glasgow and Clyde will be handled,

00:03:04.740 --> 00:03:10.500
dealt with and responded to by the
successor NHS institutions that arise from

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:13.100
the Government’s reforms, if they
are supported by Parliament.

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:20.080
Fulton MacGregor: A constituent who is a
specialist domestic abuse advocacy worker

00:03:20.080 --> 00:03:20.646
with the advocacy, support,
safety, information and services

00:03:20.646 --> 00:03:23.040
together—ASSIST—project, which is Glasgow
City Council’s domestic abuse court

00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:26.920
advocacy service, has raised serious
concerns with me about proposed changes to

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:31.120
pay and grading that could result in her
salary being reduced by around 23 per cent

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:35.180
from April 2028. Given the Scottish
Government’s ongoing commitment to

00:03:35.180 --> 00:03:38.700
tackling violence against women and girls,
what discussions will the First Minister

00:03:38.700 --> 00:03:43.060
have with Glasgow City Council to ensure
that changes to the pay and grading of

00:03:43.060 --> 00:03:46.920
specialist front-line staff do not
undermine the provision of vital support

00:03:46.920 --> 00:03:48.420
to victims of domestic abuse?

00:03:50.340 --> 00:03:55.120
The First Minister: I want to separate
two issues that Mr MacGregor raises in his

00:03:55.120 --> 00:04:01.040
question to me. The first relates to
the pay and grading changes that have

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:03.300
been taken forward by
Glasgow City Council.

00:04:03.760 --> 00:04:07.400
Those are matters for Glasgow City
Council to address as the employer of the

00:04:07.400 --> 00:04:08.600
individuals affected.

00:04:09.140 --> 00:04:14.320
I am aware of the points that Mr MacGregor
raises on the specific implications

00:04:14.320 --> 00:04:19.800
for the ASSIST organisation,
but, fundamentally, that

00:04:20.480 --> 00:04:23.600
issue is about the grading of staff
and the pay that is allocated,

00:04:23.980 --> 00:04:26.080
which is a matter for the city council.

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:32.320
The other issue is about the sustained
funding to advocacy organisations and

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:38.160
organisations that have a
responsibility to tackle

00:04:38.160 --> 00:04:39.540
violence against women and girls.

00:04:39.760 --> 00:04:43.200
As I set out in the programme
for government two weeks ago,

00:04:43.640 --> 00:04:46.940
the Government is committed to ensuring
that sustained funding is in place for

00:04:46.940 --> 00:04:47.940
those organisations.

00:04:48.440 --> 00:04:50.340
I will say more about that in
the debate this afternoon.

00:04:55.120 --> 00:04:58.720
Adam Harley: Lord Jim Wallace spent the
final years of his life campaigning with

00:04:58.720 --> 00:05:03.280
the Aortic Dissection Charitable Trust,
before sadly dying from the condition

00:05:03.280 --> 00:05:08.640
earlier this year. Aortic dissection kills
more than 2,000 people every year across

00:05:08.640 --> 00:05:12.240
the UK, and that number is
expected to double by 2050.

00:05:13.400 --> 00:05:17.060
Greater awareness of the symptoms and
a standardised pathway could improve

00:05:17.060 --> 00:05:20.080
prevention, treatment and
aftercare, and it would save lives.

00:05:20.740 --> 00:05:24.480
Scotland has three specialist centres
currently treating the condition,

00:05:24.760 --> 00:05:27.040
so we have a real
opportunity to lead the way.

00:05:27.760 --> 00:05:32.540
In memory of Jim Wallace, will the First
Minister commit to publishing national

00:05:32.540 --> 00:05:38.300
guidelines on the prevention,
diagnosis, treatment and aftercare of

00:05:38.540 --> 00:05:39.540
aortic dissection?

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.680
The First Minister: There are many ways in
which we could pay tribute to the legacy

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:51.280
and work of Jim Wallace, but Mr Harley
puts an outstanding example on the

00:05:51.280 --> 00:05:55.120
parliamentary record, and I very much
associate myself with his comments.

00:05:55.340 --> 00:06:00.480
He will know that I had the greatest of
respect for Jim Wallace and his tremendous

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.100
contribution to our public life.

00:06:04.260 --> 00:06:08.080
Neil Gray, when he was the health
secretary, met the Aortic Dissection

00:06:08.080 --> 00:06:09.240
Charitable Trust and

00:06:10.980 --> 00:06:14.000
engaged in dialogue about the
issues that Mr Harley raises.

00:06:14.740 --> 00:06:15.920
He makes a good point

00:06:17.880 --> 00:06:23.860
that, where conditions require a
clear pathway, we must make sure

00:06:23.860 --> 00:06:25.880
that those pathways exist in Scotland.

00:06:25.880 --> 00:06:29.180
I am obviously very focused on making
sure that, wherever people live in the

00:06:29.180 --> 00:06:32.840
country—I have rehearsed this point with
some of Mr Harley’s colleagues in relation

00:06:32.840 --> 00:06:35.680
to other aspects of the health
service—people can have access to

00:06:35.680 --> 00:06:36.440
that pathway.

00:06:36.440 --> 00:06:40.560
We will give active consideration
to the point that Mr Harley raises,

00:06:40.560 --> 00:06:42.040
and I will ask the Cabinet
Secretary for Health and Care,

00:06:42.040 --> 00:06:43.880
Angela Constance, to engage further.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:48.120
She would be happy to meet Mr Harley and
the trust to take forward those issues.

00:06:50.980 --> 00:06:55.560
Craig Hoy: Over the summer, residents
and business owners contacted me about a

00:06:55.560 --> 00:06:59.920
recent problem of young people clambering
on the roofs of buildings in Dumfries

00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:04.420
town centre. Known as urban
exploring, that dangerous trend,

00:07:04.420 --> 00:07:09.540
which is promoted via social media, is of
concern to residents and could cost lives.

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:14.700
Will the First Minister join me and local
police in highlighting the dangers and

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:18.780
consequences of urban exploring, and will
the Scottish Government commit to working

00:07:18.780 --> 00:07:23.220
with agencies, including Police Scotland,
to tackle that life-threatening and

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.840
concerning form of antisocial behaviour?

00:07:25.940 --> 00:07:29.420
The First Minister: I understand the
concern that Mr Hoy raises on behalf of

00:07:29.420 --> 00:07:34.980
his constituents. It is another
example—I will say more about this in the

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:40.680
debate this afternoon—of the dangerous
practices and behaviours that can be

00:07:40.680 --> 00:07:45.240
legitimised, or aired, by social media.

00:07:45.980 --> 00:07:50.580
I echo the concerns that Mr Hoy expresses,
and I commit the Government to working

00:07:50.580 --> 00:07:52.560
with and supporting Police Scotland.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:57.180
I am certain that there will be good
engagement with Police Scotland at a local

00:07:57.180 --> 00:08:01.860
level, but there will be a necessity for
engagement with other public authorities,

00:08:02.100 --> 00:08:05.400
perhaps including Dumfries and Galloway
Council, in relation to the education

00:08:05.400 --> 00:08:06.860
service in that community.

00:08:07.100 --> 00:08:10.280
There will be a wider application;
I cannot imagine that it is only an

00:08:10.280 --> 00:08:16.260
issue in Dumfries. Mr Hoy raises a point
on which it is important to capture

00:08:16.260 --> 00:08:17.260
public attention.

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:25.740
Katherine Sangster: Teaching unions warn
that school support staff are not being

00:08:25.740 --> 00:08:29.740
supported to deal with increasing
instances of violent behaviour by primary

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:33.210
school pupils. Last year, the City of
Edinburgh Council paid out more than

00:08:33.210 --> 00:08:35.340
£250,000 in damages to support staff.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:41.300
It has warned that, given the
black hole of £650 million in

00:08:41.300 --> 00:08:44.060
local government finances left
by Government underfunding,

00:08:44.520 --> 00:08:46.580
it does not have the resources
to deal with the problem.

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.700
What action will the First Minister take
to ensure that teaching support staff are

00:08:51.700 --> 00:08:53.280
not left to face the issue alone?

00:08:54.980 --> 00:09:00.540
The First Minister: The answer to those
issues is to ensure that behaviour in our

00:09:00.540 --> 00:09:05.760
schools, whether it is encountered
by teaching support staff,

00:09:06.760 --> 00:09:11.540
teaching staff or pupils, is
appropriate and satisfactory.

00:09:12.560 --> 00:09:17.620
The Government has set out a range of
measures over some time to ensure that

00:09:17.620 --> 00:09:20.320
behaviour issues are confronted
in the school system.

00:09:20.640 --> 00:09:25.640
We are putting in place resources,
supporting local authorities and working

00:09:25.640 --> 00:09:30.580
with professional associations and other
trade unions to ensure that members of

00:09:30.580 --> 00:09:31.740
staff are supported at all times.

00:09:31.740 --> 00:09:37.700
Katherine Sangster’s point about the
importance of maintaining acceptable

00:09:37.700 --> 00:09:42.520
behaviour in our schools for the
safety of everyone is one that I

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.060
endorse unreservedly.

00:09:46.420 --> 00:09:50.140
Maggie Chapman: The First Minister will
be aware that NHS Tayside plans to close

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:53.800
Kingsway care centre in Dundee
in only four months’ time,

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:57.820
moving dementia assessment and treatment
to Forfar and cutting the number of beds

00:09:57.820 --> 00:10:03.280
by 36 per cent. Vulnerable adults
with psychosis, depression or other

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:06.580
conditions will also have
their care relocated.

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.580
What does the First Minister say to
families and carers who might face

00:10:10.580 --> 00:10:16.560
multiple bus journeys—if suitable services
even exist—to support elderly people

00:10:16.560 --> 00:10:18.900
with dementia simply to access care?

00:10:19.420 --> 00:10:22.400
Will the First Minister ensure that
no changes proceed without proper

00:10:22.400 --> 00:10:26.988
consultation, a full equality impact
assessment and clear evidence that there

00:10:26.988 --> 00:10:28.780
will be no detriment to patient care?

00:10:30.760 --> 00:10:35.900
The First Minister: It is my expectation
that, before NHS Tayside embarked on any

00:10:35.900 --> 00:10:41.540
such move, the implications
for individual residents were

00:10:41.540 --> 00:10:45.660
taken into account in all
the steps that were taken.

00:10:46.240 --> 00:10:51.460
For any patient whose care
arrangements are being altered,

00:10:53.440 --> 00:10:58.340
an individual assessment has to be
undertaken to ensure that their needs are

00:10:58.340 --> 00:10:59.960
properly taken into account.

00:11:00.700 --> 00:11:06.440
That is my expectation of NHS
Tayside, and I will ask the Cabinet

00:11:06.440 --> 00:11:10.460
Secretary for Health and Social Care to
make that point to the health board.

00:11:10.460 --> 00:11:15.220
There are occasions when it is
necessary to redesign services,

00:11:15.220 --> 00:11:17.200
which we must all accept.

00:11:17.860 --> 00:11:21.860
At some stage—I have dealt with this
extensively in my constituency experience

00:11:22.860 --> 00:11:28.800
over the years—we have to face
up to the need to modernise and

00:11:28.800 --> 00:11:30.620
change the way that we deliver services.

00:11:33.260 --> 00:11:36.480
As we set out in the programme for
government, that can only be done in a

00:11:36.480 --> 00:11:40.220
patient-centred fashion that
addresses each individual’s needs.

00:11:40.220 --> 00:11:41.900
That is my expectation in that case.

00:11:47.180 --> 00:11:51.680
Angela Ross: Thursday’s high-pressure gas
leak at the planned battery storage site

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:56.540
in Cockenzie, East Lothian, has reinforced
local concerns about major energy and

00:11:56.540 --> 00:12:01.700
digital infrastructure developments being
located in close proximity to homes,

00:12:02.020 --> 00:12:03.180
schools and public spaces.

00:12:03.180 --> 00:12:07.220
Although the incident was not caused by
the battery installation itself but by

00:12:07.220 --> 00:12:11.280
work at the site, does it not highlight
the need to review the national planning

00:12:11.280 --> 00:12:16.140
framework to ensure that such developments
are directed to suitable locations and

00:12:16.140 --> 00:12:20.200
that the impact on existing
communities is given greater weight in

00:12:20.200 --> 00:12:21.200
planning decisions?

00:12:23.860 --> 00:12:28.480
The First Minister: In coming to any
decision about the location of facilities,

00:12:28.480 --> 00:12:34.200
there has to be an assessment of all
relevant factors in each locality and

00:12:34.200 --> 00:12:39.780
an open and rigorous assessment of
any implications that might be felt by

00:12:39.780 --> 00:12:42.080
the local community as a consequence.

00:12:42.340 --> 00:12:45.520
That is an essential requirement
in all our planning policies.

00:12:46.240 --> 00:12:51.500
The national planning framework sets out
at a strategic level the approach that is

00:12:51.500 --> 00:12:57.160
taken in relation to the delineation
of land for particular uses and

00:12:57.160 --> 00:13:00.599
the specific considerations that
have to be borne in mind when

00:13:00.599 --> 00:13:02.340
progressing any development.

00:13:02.620 --> 00:13:07.760
All those matters are considered
in the planning process.

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:13.940
I reiterate one other point, which is
that, whenever work is undertaken at any

00:13:13.940 --> 00:13:19.620
site, the importance of public safety
and the safety of the workforce

00:13:19.620 --> 00:13:22.280
that is involved is paramount.

00:13:22.280 --> 00:13:25.040
That requires interaction with the

00:13:26.660 --> 00:13:29.440
Health and Safety Executive’s regulatory
powers, which should be applied in all

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:30.460
appropriate circumstances.

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:39.160
Calum Kerr: Yesterday, First Ministers
across these islands met in Cardiff to

00:13:39.160 --> 00:13:44.200
sign a memorandum of understanding that
our nations should have the right to

00:13:44.200 --> 00:13:47.460
decide our own constitutional future.

00:13:48.220 --> 00:13:53.520
Last week, the United Kingdom Prime
Minister also agreed the principle that

00:13:53.520 --> 00:13:58.200
Scotland should have a referendum
when it is likely that people would

00:13:58.200 --> 00:13:59.680
vote for independence.

00:14:00.360 --> 00:14:05.640
Given that, what is the next step in the
First Minister’s plan to build on the

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:06.640
Cardiff agreement?

00:14:12.650 --> 00:14:16.550
The First Minister: Yesterday was a
welcome opportunity for me to meet the

00:14:16.550 --> 00:14:20.810
First Ministers of Wales and Northern
Ireland on the important point that Mr

00:14:20.810 --> 00:14:26.210
Kerr raises—that the people of this
country should be free to decide their own

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:27.390
constitutional future.

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:32.410
I welcome the comments that the Prime
Minister made in the House of Commons last

00:14:32.410 --> 00:14:36.270
Wednesday when he recognised the
centrality of that principle.

00:14:36.470 --> 00:14:40.650
It is a fundamental point of
self-determination, and I was glad to have

00:14:40.650 --> 00:14:43.150
the opportunity to reinforce
that point yesterday.

00:14:43.830 --> 00:14:47.690
As for the next steps that I intend to
take, I made it clear yesterday that,

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:51.970
during this parliamentary session, I want
Scotland to have the opportunity to decide

00:14:51.970 --> 00:14:55.310
on our constitutional future through
a referendum on independence.

00:14:55.950 --> 00:15:00.850
I will make sure that the efforts of my
office—and those of Government—are put

00:15:00.850 --> 00:15:04.110
into ensuring that that is the case and
that the people of Scotland are free to

00:15:04.110 --> 00:15:06.670
decide their own future by 2031.

00:15:15.000 --> 00:15:18.180
Kristopher Leask: The First Minister has
long recognised the importance of flight

00:15:18.180 --> 00:15:20.180
connections to communities in
the Highlands and Islands,

00:15:20.180 --> 00:15:21.787
and I was glad to see the
Cabinet Secretary for Economy,

00:15:21.787 --> 00:15:23.920
Tourism and Transport call a regional
aviation stakeholder meeting.

00:15:24.620 --> 00:15:27.000
However, given that the cabinet
secretary is not attending the meeting,

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:30.120
which he called, to hear directly from
stakeholders in the Highlands and Islands,

00:15:30.420 --> 00:15:31.940
will the First Minister
attend in his place?

00:15:35.980 --> 00:15:40.020
The First Minister: The cabinet secretary
has already intervened on flight issues,

00:15:40.020 --> 00:15:42.360
and I am very pleased with the
outcome that he has managed to secure,

00:15:42.360 --> 00:15:48.320
in order to sustain a number
of services that are being

00:15:48.320 --> 00:15:49.320
taken forward.

00:15:49.640 --> 00:15:54.860
In relation to the forum, the discussion
involves representatives of the

00:15:54.860 --> 00:16:00.360
Government—all the information that is
aired will be fed back to ministers,

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:03.860
and ministers will take the appropriate
decisions in response to those questions.

00:16:03.860 --> 00:16:09.220
I take this opportunity to reinforce
the importance that I attach to flight

00:16:09.220 --> 00:16:12.920
connections to our island communities, and
I know that that point is reinforced by

00:16:12.920 --> 00:16:14.080
the work of the cabinet secretary.

00:16:17.820 --> 00:16:21.680
Jenni Minto: Tomorrow, Chest Heart &
Stroke Scotland launches its new women’s

00:16:21.680 --> 00:16:25.800
health plan. Given that women are 50 per
cent more likely than men to receive an

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:30.560
initial misdiagnosis following a heart
attack and continue to experience poorer

00:16:30.560 --> 00:16:31.880
cardiovascular outcomes,

00:16:33.440 --> 00:16:38.080
what action is the Scottish Government
taking, through phase 2 of the Scottish

00:16:38.080 --> 00:16:42.340
Government’s women’s health plan, to
improve women’s cardiovascular health and

00:16:42.340 --> 00:16:45.440
build on existing commitments
to reduce those inequalities?

00:16:46.820 --> 00:16:51.240
The First Minister: First, I recognise
the important work that Jenni Minto did,

00:16:51.240 --> 00:16:54.540
in her time as a minister, to
advance the women’s health plan.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:58.740
As she will be aware, phase 2 of the
women’s health plan aims to address the

00:16:58.740 --> 00:17:01.580
inequalities that affect the health
of all women and girls in Scotland.

00:17:02.560 --> 00:17:06.580
The cardiovascular disease risk factors
programme has a dedicated women’s health

00:17:06.580 --> 00:17:11.100
workstream that is focused on reducing
lifetime cardiovascular risk in women with

00:17:11.100 --> 00:17:13.160
hypertensive disorders of pregnancy.

00:17:13.820 --> 00:17:17.800
Support and resources have been developed
with a variety of third sector partners to

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:19.180
ensure that they meet the needs of women.

00:17:19.180 --> 00:17:23.140
Early findings from testing sites
suggest that the pathway of care is

00:17:23.140 --> 00:17:27.100
functioning well. In addition, training
has been delivered in the national health

00:17:27.100 --> 00:17:29.000
service to improve awareness among
healthcare professionals of the

00:17:29.000 --> 00:17:33.260
interaction between women’s reproductive
health and future cardiovascular risk.

00:17:35.660 --> 00:17:37.280
Morven-May MacCallum: I refer members

00:17:40.340 --> 00:17:41.260
to my entry in the register of members’
interests, which states that I am a

00:17:41.260 --> 00:17:42.620
councillor with Highland Council.

00:17:43.500 --> 00:17:48.800
The First Minister will possibly
have seen the story of Sophie McNab,

00:17:48.800 --> 00:17:52.560
a young girl from Inverness who
is in need of residential care.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:58.580
Sophie has been placed in Annan,
some 230 miles from her home

00:17:58.580 --> 00:18:01.780
and her family. Her case is not unique.

00:18:02.540 --> 00:18:07.540
There are currently 26 Highland children
living far from home in Dumfries and

00:18:07.540 --> 00:18:10.520
Galloway, North Lanarkshire,
West Lothian and elsewhere.

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:14.520
Why? Because there is not suitable
provision for them in Highland.

00:18:15.090 --> 00:18:18.840
Does the First Minister agree that
children being separated from their

00:18:18.840 --> 00:18:20.260
families in that way is unacceptable?

00:18:21.100 --> 00:18:22.520
Will his Government bring them home?

00:18:25.380 --> 00:18:28.533
The First Minister: I agree with the
fundamental point that Morven-May

00:18:28.533 --> 00:18:34.180
MacCallum made in the conclusion of
her question—that it is best for

00:18:34.180 --> 00:18:37.640
children to be supported in

00:18:39.180 --> 00:18:43.780
their own family circumstances if doing so
would be safe and sustainable and that,

00:18:43.780 --> 00:18:48.300
if it would not be, they should be
supported as close to home as possible.

00:18:48.620 --> 00:18:51.100
That is an inherent element
of the Promise, to which

00:18:53.300 --> 00:18:54.700
the Government is absolutely committed.

00:18:55.360 --> 00:18:57.440
We work closely with local authorities.

00:18:57.440 --> 00:19:02.920
Morven-May MacCallum
made the important point

00:19:04.440 --> 00:19:09.740
that, although there may be a number of
children and young people in the Highlands

00:19:09.740 --> 00:19:13.980
and Islands who require that support,
there may not be such provision in the

00:19:13.980 --> 00:19:15.260
individual locality.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:21.760
That is one reason why we must look
to undertake the necessary regional

00:19:21.760 --> 00:19:27.260
work to make sure that there is
provision that better meets the needs

00:19:27.260 --> 00:19:29.000
of individual young people.

00:19:29.000 --> 00:19:33.100
Siobhian Brown, the Minister for
Children, Young People and The Promise,

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:38.585
would be happy to engage further on the
specific problem in the Highlands that the

00:19:38.585 --> 00:19:42.420
member raises and to discuss it with the
representatives of Highland Council who

00:19:42.420 --> 00:19:43.700
have responsibility in that area.

00:19:47.300 --> 00:19:50.460
Miles Briggs: Last Monday, the Scottish
Medicines Consortium approved two

00:19:50.460 --> 00:19:56.360
promising new

00:19:56.360 --> 00:20:01.680
drugs for metastatic breast cancer,
which offer real hope for those who are

00:20:01.680 --> 00:20:03.820
suffering from the disease
before it has progressed.

00:20:03.820 --> 00:20:07.770
In England, those drugs and the necessary
genomic tests have been available for more

00:20:07.770 --> 00:20:11.460
than a year. However, increasingly,
patients in Scotland cannot access the

00:20:11.460 --> 00:20:15.700
drugs because the genomic tests that
establish whether someone is suitable to

00:20:15.700 --> 00:20:18.860
receive them are not routinely
available through NHS Scotland.

00:20:19.240 --> 00:20:20.740
That issue affects not only
breast cancer treatment.

00:20:20.740 --> 00:20:25.720
Since 2021, at least 15 other cancer
medicines have been restricted because of

00:20:25.720 --> 00:20:27.360
a lack of availability of testing.

00:20:27.360 --> 00:20:32.020
Can the First Minister provide an update
on what interim solution will be put in

00:20:32.020 --> 00:20:34.660
place for patients who are looking
to access those treatments?

00:20:35.160 --> 00:20:38.440
Does the First Minister agree that
Scotland needs a sustainable,

00:20:38.440 --> 00:20:41.220
long-term model for genomic testing?

00:20:51.620 --> 00:20:52.763
The First Minister: The availability of
treatments is actively being explored by

00:20:52.763 --> 00:20:56.482
the Cabinet Secretary for Health and Care
in relation to the outcome of the SMC’s

00:20:56.482 --> 00:20:58.715
consideration of the issue.

00:20:58.715 --> 00:21:04.480
On the point about genomic testing, I was
involved in a discussion last week on a

00:21:04.480 --> 00:21:09.740
separate issue—not in relation to
breast cancer—in which we explored the

00:21:09.740 --> 00:21:13.280
strengthening of our capability
for genomic testing,

00:21:13.280 --> 00:21:18.920
and I convey encouragement to Mr Briggs

00:21:20.060 --> 00:21:21.420
that good progress has been made.

00:21:23.000 --> 00:21:27.620
That conversation involved a briefing
from the chief scientist for health,

00:21:27.620 --> 00:21:33.120
Dame Anna Dominiczak, who is leading
significant work to advance in that area.

00:21:36.440 --> 00:21:40.340
The specific issue is being explored, and
significant progress is being made on the

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:43.720
general issue of the testing
and assessment arrangements.

00:21:47.100 --> 00:21:52.280
I will write to Mr Briggs with
information, to address his interest and

00:21:52.280 --> 00:21:54.360
reassure him that progress is being made.

00:21:56.800 --> 00:21:59.560
Pauline Stafford: The events over
the weekend in Armadale, in my

00:21:59.560 --> 00:22:03.540
constituency—with the death of a
35-year-old man, reportedly following a

00:22:03.540 --> 00:22:08.060
targeted attack and the suspected use of a
firearm—have deeply shocked the community.

00:22:08.700 --> 00:22:11.440
My thoughts are with the man’s family and
all those who are affected at this time.

00:22:11.840 --> 00:22:14.320
I appreciate that the police
investigation is ongoing.

00:22:14.320 --> 00:22:17.820
However, can the First Minister provide
my constituents with reassurance that the

00:22:17.820 --> 00:22:21.900
Scottish Government and Police Scotland
are taking all necessary steps to ensure

00:22:21.900 --> 00:22:23.000
the safety of the community?

00:22:24.300 --> 00:22:28.380
The First Minister: I was sorry to hear
about the incident at the weekend and I

00:22:28.380 --> 00:22:29.860
appreciate the opportunity

00:22:31.800 --> 00:22:33.340
to comment on the issue.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:37.320
As Pauline Stafford indicates, there
is a live police investigation.

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:39.340
I am advised that, with the expertise that
comes from its being the national police

00:22:39.340 --> 00:22:40.720
force of Scotland, Police
Scotland has available all the

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:46.940
necessary resources to explore
and examine the case and

00:22:52.820 --> 00:22:54.860
to enable those issues to be addressed.

00:22:55.520 --> 00:23:01.520
I reassure Pauline Stafford and her
constituents that issues of public

00:23:01.520 --> 00:23:05.620
safety are uppermost in Police Scotland’s
consideration and in its reassuring of

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:06.620
members of the public.

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:13.127
Liam McArthur: This week, Movember
published a comprehensive report that

00:23:13.127 --> 00:23:16.900
explored the leading causes of premature
mortality among men in Scotland.

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:21.440
“The Real Face of Men’s Health” shows that
men born in Scotland currently have the

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:26.620
lowest life expectancy of men in any
United Kingdom nation—77.4 years—and that

00:23:26.620 --> 00:23:30.900
socioeconomic factors are contributing to
significant disparities in men’s health

00:23:30.900 --> 00:23:35.020
across the country. Does the First
Minister share my alarm at those findings?

00:23:35.220 --> 00:23:40.280
If so, what action will he take to improve
outcomes for men’s health across every

00:23:40.280 --> 00:23:41.320
community in Scotland?

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:46.840
The First Minister: I am grateful to
Mr McArthur for asking that question,

00:23:46.840 --> 00:23:52.580
because the report provides a timely
reminder of some of the challenges that we

00:23:52.580 --> 00:23:57.780
face relating to men’s health and the need
to improve outcomes relating to men’s

00:23:57.780 --> 00:23:58.780
health and wellbeing.

00:24:00.920 --> 00:24:03.480
The Government’s programme includes
a heavy focus on shifting to early

00:24:03.480 --> 00:24:04.960
intervention and prevention.

00:24:05.160 --> 00:24:11.020
That means intervening on issues
related to alcohol and drug

00:24:11.020 --> 00:24:16.820
use, and it means encouraging
and supporting men to live

00:24:16.820 --> 00:24:22.380
healthier lifestyles so that they have
the greatest capacity to deal with

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:27.520
the risk of heart disease,
stroke, lung cancer

00:24:27.520 --> 00:24:30.120
and poor mental wellbeing.

00:24:30.120 --> 00:24:34.040
The fundamental shift in our public
services to early intervention and

00:24:34.040 --> 00:24:37.780
prevention is designed to address
the important issues that Mr

00:24:37.780 --> 00:24:39.220
McArthur has raised.

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:46.140
Amanda Lindsay: Will the First Minister
stop his Government smearing women who

00:24:46.140 --> 00:24:51.980
protested at Glasgow Green over the
weekend as “far right” and instead tell us

00:24:51.980 --> 00:24:56.850
what he will do to address the failures
on women’s safety that took those

00:24:56.850 --> 00:24:57.850
women there?

00:25:00.300 --> 00:25:03.260
The First Minister: I will be leading a
debate this afternoon on violence against

00:25:03.260 --> 00:25:08.900
women and girls, and I want
to have an open and candid

00:25:08.900 --> 00:25:11.680
discussion of those important issues.

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:17.680
However, I also say to Amanda Lindsay that
I will never ever stop confronting the far

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:18.680
right in this country.

00:25:27.190 --> 00:25:30.610
Lloyd Melville: Dundee and Angus College
has ambitious plans for a new campus at

00:25:30.610 --> 00:25:35.010
Arbroath, which would be the biggest
education and regeneration project in the

00:25:35.010 --> 00:25:37.890
town’s history. The Scottish National
Party manifesto committed the Government

00:25:37.890 --> 00:25:41.010
to supporting Dundee and Angus
College to renew its estate.

00:25:41.010 --> 00:25:45.370
Will the First Minister give an update on
the work that is being undertaken with the

00:25:45.370 --> 00:25:48.150
Scottish Funding Council to
drive that project forward?

00:25:53.730 --> 00:25:57.123
The First Minister: Dundee and Angus
College has ambitious plans, as Mr

00:25:57.123 --> 00:26:00.790
Melville highlighted, that will affect
the city of Dundee and Arbroath.

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:06.030
The Government has provided the Funding
Council with £2 million for a first-wave

00:26:06.030 --> 00:26:10.170
group of colleges to develop business
cases for transformational capital

00:26:10.170 --> 00:26:11.230
investment projects.

00:26:11.230 --> 00:26:16.430
Dundee and Angus College received £700,000
of additional funding from that money to

00:26:16.430 --> 00:26:18.670
help it to determine the
best option for renewal.

00:26:19.110 --> 00:26:22.250
Through the work of the Funding Council,
the Government will stay close to the

00:26:22.250 --> 00:26:23.250
project’s developments.

00:26:26.110 --> 00:26:30.930
Martyn Day: The First Minister will be
aware of the 53 to 54 per cent success

00:26:30.930 --> 00:26:34.970
rate for Motability appeals, with
tribunal cases often taking between 12

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:40.128
and 18 months. What representations will
Scottish ministers make to their United

00:26:40.128 --> 00:26:44.710
Kingdom Government counterparts to try to
reduce appeal delays that leave successful

00:26:44.710 --> 00:26:47.850
claimants without a Motability
car for up to 18 months?

00:26:49.430 --> 00:26:53.710
The First Minister: We have made
representations to the UK Government on

00:26:53.710 --> 00:26:54.810
that important issue.

00:26:54.810 --> 00:27:00.630
When Motability cars

00:27:00.630 --> 00:27:06.570
are required by individuals—in many cases,
that is the only way in which individuals

00:27:06.570 --> 00:27:07.570
are able

00:27:09.250 --> 00:27:12.950
to move within their communities—we want
to ensure that individuals are supported

00:27:12.950 --> 00:27:14.650
in a timely fashion.

00:27:15.370 --> 00:27:21.030
Mr Day’s question will prompt me to ensure
that the relevant minister makes further

00:27:21.030 --> 00:27:22.930
representations to the UK Government.

00:27:25.270 --> 00:27:29.290
Holly Bruce: The site of Scotland’s
first national football ground,

00:27:29.290 --> 00:27:33.910
which is a vital community space, sits
in my Glasgow Southside constituency.

00:27:33.910 --> 00:27:39.070
A group of neighbours known as First
Hampden is fighting to keep the site as a

00:27:39.070 --> 00:27:42.870
community hub and a football
heritage site that is open to all.

00:27:43.510 --> 00:27:48.770
Does the First Minister agree that that
space should be protected and run by the

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:51.870
community instead of being sold
off to the highest bidder?

00:27:56.390 --> 00:27:59.770
The First Minister: I am sympathetic to
the issue that Holly Bruce has raised.

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:03.790
I had better ensure that I
give her an accurate answer on

00:28:06.430 --> 00:28:10.010
the relevant issues that are being
wrestled with in relation to ownership and

00:28:10.010 --> 00:28:14.090
use, so I will write to her in due course
with details of what the Government can do

00:28:14.090 --> 00:28:15.090
in that case.

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:20.520
David Linden: In his earlier response to
Liam McArthur, the First Minister made

00:28:20.520 --> 00:28:24.820
reference to the importance of
men’s health and, in particular,

00:28:24.820 --> 00:28:25.940
to the Movember report.

00:28:26.520 --> 00:28:30.060
I am sure that the First Minister will be
aware that places such as men’s sheds are

00:28:30.060 --> 00:28:33.740
particularly important in
shining a subtle, yet bright,

00:28:33.740 --> 00:28:34.740
light on those issues.

00:28:35.160 --> 00:28:38.620
I therefore press the First Minister to
come to visit Shettleston Men’s Shed to

00:28:38.620 --> 00:28:42.700
see the importance of such community
spaces in advocating on issues pertaining

00:28:42.700 --> 00:28:43.700
to men’s health.

00:28:45.900 --> 00:28:50.020
The First Minister: I would be happy
to visit the Shettleston Men’s Shed.

00:28:50.020 --> 00:28:54.279
I have visited a number of those ventures
around the country—most recently,

00:28:54.279 --> 00:28:57.580
if memory serves me right, in Renfrew, in
the constituency of my colleague Michelle

00:28:57.580 --> 00:29:01.540
Campbell—so I have
constituency experience.

00:29:02.100 --> 00:29:07.880
Men’s sheds are very helpful in
encouraging men to meet and perhaps to

00:29:07.880 --> 00:29:12.800
overcome some of the challenges that
they face due to personal isolation.

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:18.240
It is a tremendous movement that has
made a welcome impact on the lives of

00:29:18.240 --> 00:29:22.120
men in Scotland. I am glad to hear that
there is a vibrant organisation in

00:29:22.120 --> 00:29:26.680
Shettleston and look forward to finding
the opportunity to visit with Mr Linden.

00:29:29.740 --> 00:29:33.200
Michael Marra: The First Minister will be
aware of the severe issues that women in

00:29:33.200 --> 00:29:35.880
Tayside face in accessing
gynaecology services.

00:29:36.440 --> 00:29:39.877
NHS Tayside has told the Scottish
Government that the number of women who

00:29:39.877 --> 00:29:43.520
are waiting for more than a year
has reduced from 1,348 to 11,

00:29:43.520 --> 00:29:48.640
but those figures are simply not true and
I have many constituents who have been

00:29:48.640 --> 00:29:49.800
waiting for multiple years.

00:29:50.080 --> 00:29:53.220
One constituent was told that she
would wait for more than three years

00:29:53.220 --> 00:29:57.300
for a hysterectomy. She took out a loan
and went private and she has never made it

00:29:57.300 --> 00:29:59.040
on to the waiting list as a result.

00:29:59.360 --> 00:30:03.180
Does the First Minister believe that those
terrible waits in Tayside are acceptable?

00:30:05.300 --> 00:30:10.700
The First Minister: I basically take the
view that waiting times must be reduced

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:15.080
wherever they are. Mr Marra raises an
important point about gynaecology services

00:30:15.080 --> 00:30:16.160
in Tayside

00:30:18.260 --> 00:30:20.280
and I want to see those
waiting times reduced.

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:25.740
I will go away and look at the statistics
that he has raised doubts about,

00:30:26.300 --> 00:30:28.960
because it is important that
all those details are accurate.

00:30:31.180 --> 00:30:34.375
The Cabinet Secretary for Health and Care
and I look at waiting list levels weekly,

00:30:34.375 --> 00:30:35.375
doing so by

00:30:37.100 --> 00:30:39.800
discipline and locality
around the country.

00:30:40.440 --> 00:30:42.900
There is variation: in
some parts of the country

00:30:44.940 --> 00:30:48.676
there is very good performance on
waiting times in certain disciplines;

00:30:48.676 --> 00:30:50.860
in other parts of the country,
in the same discipline,

00:30:51.080 --> 00:30:52.520
that is not the case.

00:30:52.940 --> 00:30:56.200
That was one of the issues raised
by David Green a few weeks ago.

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:59.740
That is one reason why the
Government is pursuing its

00:31:01.920 --> 00:31:03.180
national health service reform programme.

00:31:03.180 --> 00:31:08.360
We want to remove the obstructions that
health board boundaries can sometimes put

00:31:08.360 --> 00:31:13.220
in the way of ensuring that the type
of equity that Mr Marra seeks for his

00:31:13.220 --> 00:31:15.020
constituents can actually be delivered.

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:23.200
I will look at those particular figures,
but I assure Mr Marra that there is

00:31:23.200 --> 00:31:27.040
sustained interrogation, on a weekly
basis, by the health secretary and me,

00:31:27.040 --> 00:31:31.100
of the steps that are being taken to
reduce waiting lists and waiting times.

00:31:31.100 --> 00:31:34.844
He will understand from what I have said
from this podium for some time that I

00:31:34.844 --> 00:31:37.020
attach the greatest of
importance to that issue.

00:31:38.180 --> 00:31:39.380
The Presiding Officer:
Thank you, First Minister.

00:31:39.880 --> 00:31:42.900
I apologise to the 28 backbench
members I was unable to call today.
